": "sha256:b791ea80c8bdb1484e0929828796ceb54d7e38fff1f675f1d2275c773979f01d", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:131b363c333c08f1c7f0b381acbec5c0403abe47fe5501808491db08f12edc4b", "source_text": "\n\n<STRUCT_0001>\nIn this section we explain how to get the sheafification\nof a presheaf on a topological space. We will use stalks\nto describe the sheafification in this case. This is different\nfrom the general procedure described in Sites, Section\n<REF_0001>, and perhaps somewhat\neasier to understand.\n\n<STRUCT_0002><STRUCT_0003>\nThe basic construction is the following. Let <MATH_0001> be a presheaf\nof sets on a topological space <MATH_0002>.\nFor every open <MATH_0003> we define\n<MATHSEG_0001> such that <MATHSEG_0002>\nwhere <MATH_0004> is the property:\n<STRUCT_0004>\n<ITEMOPEN_0001>(*)<ITEMCLOSE_0001> For every <MATH_0005>, there exists an open neighbourhood\n<MATH_0006>, and a section <MATH_0007>\nsuch that for all <MATH_0008> we have <MATH_0009>\nin <MATH_0010>.\n<STRUCT_0005>\nNote that <MATH_0011> is a condition for each <MATH_0012>,\nand that given <MATH_0013> the truth of this condition\ndepends only on the values <MATH_0014> for <MATH_0015> in any open neighbourhood\nof <MATH_0016>. Thus it is clear that,\nif <MATH_0017> are open, the projection maps\n<MATH_0018>\nmaps elements of <MATH_0019> into <MATH_0020>.\nUsing these maps as the restriction mappings, we turn <MATH_0021>\ninto a presheaf of sets on <MATH_0022>.\n\n<STRUCT_0006><STRUCT_0007>\nFurthermore, the map <MATH_0023>\ndescribed in Section <REF_0002> clearly has image\nin <MATH_0024>. In addition, if <MATH_0025> are\nopen then we have the following commutative diagram\n<MATH_0026>\nwhere the vertical maps are induced from the\nrestriction mappings. Thus we see that\nthere is a canonical morphism of presheaves\n<MATH_0027>.\n\n<STRUCT_0008><STRUCT_0009>\nIn Example <REF_0003> we saw\nthat the rule <MATH_0028>\nis a sheaf, with obvious restriction mappings. And by construction\n<MATH_0029> is a subpresheaf of this. In other words, we\nhave morphisms of presheaves\n<MATH_0030>\nIn addition the rule that associates to <MATH_0031>\nthe sequence above is clearly functorial in the presheaf <MATH_0032>.\nThis notation will be\nused in the proofs of the lemmas below.\n\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:b66ee2ed42cbc794fd4ef6f6aa6920572594343f43fdae610ceb17621f56a611", "unit_id": "tag:007X:prose-0001"}
